   234	Exception events are consistently the most effective component on both models, and their structural simplicity---a fixed-format error message---ensures that the relative advantage over richer representations holds across all seeds.
   235	Two of three DeepSeek seeds match Qwen-level accuracy (99.33\%); the outlier (seed 123, 91.09\%) is attributable to LoRA initialization landing in a suboptimal basin (fail recall = 70.5\%), not to a failure of the exception signal itself---even this outlier outperforms VOTA (84.45\%) by 6.64pp.
   236	The VOTA cross-model gap is confounded by two factors: (1)~tokenizer efficiency---the DeepSeek tokenizer averages 2{,}626 tokens per VOTA sample versus Qwen's 1{,}929, causing 91.7\% of training samples to exceed the 2{,}048-token context window, truncating trace content while preserving the verdict label---and (2)~base model capability differences, consistent with findings that SFT generalization depends on pretrain quality \citep{yang2024generalization}.
   237	The two factors cannot be disentangled in the current experimental design; a controlled study with matched tokenizer efficiency would be needed to isolate the model capability effect.
   238	
   239	\section{Training Dynamics and Loss Decoupling}
   240	\label{sec:training_dynamics_appendix}
   241	\label{sec:training_dynamics}
   242	
   243	\begin{figure*}[t]
   244	\centering
   245	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{training_dynamics}
   246	\caption{Training dynamics for \texttt{exception\_only} (blue, circles) and \texttt{full\_trace} (red, squares) on Qwen-2.5-7B (seed=42, $N{=}1{,}190$). Left: test accuracy over training steps; center: per-class F1 (solid = pass, dashed = fail); right: training loss. \texttt{exception\_only} converges to near-perfect accuracy ({\raise.17ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}99.3\%) by step~50 and remains stable, while \texttt{full\_trace} plateaus around 88\%.}
   247	\label{fig:training_dynamics}
   248	\end{figure*}
   249	
   250	The DeepSeek \texttt{exception\_only} results reveal two instructive training dynamics.
   251	First, epoch-level analysis shows mild degradation in 2/3 seeds: seed 42 peaks at 99.83\% after epoch~1 but settles at 99.33\% by epoch~3 ($-$0.50pp), seed 123 degrades from 91.43\% to 91.09\% ($-$0.34pp), while seed 456 shows no epoch degradation (99.33\% at both epoch~1 and epoch~3).
   252	Early stopping may yield marginal gains for some seeds, but the epoch effect is small (${\leq}$0.5pp) compared to the seed effect (8.24pp between seeds 42 and 123).
   253	
   254	Second, seeds 123 and 456 converge to nearly identical final training losses (0.122 and 0.123 respectively), yet their accuracies differ by 8.24pp (91.09\% vs.\ 99.33\%).
   255	The critical difference lies in fail recall: seeds 42 and 456 achieve 100\%, while seed 123 achieves only 70.5\%---the LoRA initialization in seed 123 fails to learn the exception$\to$fail mapping for a substantial fraction of failing samples, even though the per-token loss appears converged.
   256	This demonstrates that token-level training loss can mask fundamental differences in sample-level classification ability, suggesting a parallel to the eval-loss/accuracy decoupling observed in DeepSeek VOTA (where eval loss rose 65\% from epoch 1 to 3 while accuracy improved by 6.3pp) at the training phase.
   257	The practical recommendation is twofold: (1)~use task-specific metrics (accuracy, fail recall) for checkpoint and seed selection rather than training or evaluation loss, and (2)~for deployment on smaller models, train multiple seeds and select the best, as a single run may not reliably converge to the correct classification boundary.
   258	
   259	The contrast with VOTA is instructive: DeepSeek VOTA accuracy monotonically increases from 78.15\% to 84.45\% across epochs, but is consistent with a performance limit associated with the high trace truncation rate (\S\ref{sec:cross_model})---the model progressively extracts signal from samples with intact trace content but cannot fully compensate for the information loss in the truncated majority.
   260	The 84.45\% accuracy reflects a combination of trace information loss and model capability constraints.
